  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday through next Saturday)\nIssued at 111 PM CDT Sat Oct 3 2026\n\nDrier air advances southward into the region for early to midweek \nwith a northerly flow generally in place across the low to mid-\nlevels of the atmosphere. Monday may still have enough lingering \nmoisture for some cloudiness and a stray shower in the Edwards \nPlateau, otherwise rain chances come to an end with sunshine \nbecoming far more prevalent. This drier air will result in a more \nrefreshing airmass and feel featuring cooler mornings and mild \nafternoons with low humidity. The afternoon highs stay in the 80s \nfor the most part while the morning lows dip down as low as the \nupper 50s across the Hill Country and into the low to mid 60s \nelsewhere.\n\nLow rain chances (~15-20%) enter the forecast late this week into \nnext weekend but the overall forecast confidence is lower than \naverage for this time range as the latest guidance diverges \nregarding the possible evolution and also interaction between a \nweakness within the mid-level flow that may establish across the \nstate and a possible disturbance associated with the Central \nAmerican Gyre in the Bay of Campeche. Temperatures should remain \nseasonable in the morning but do slowly climb from midweek and \nafternoon highs will be warm with several nearing or reaching\nthe 90 degree mark.",
